The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says “One issue, one fix queued.” Its rendered
cause table contradicts that statement: distinct input paths produce distinct defects. Keep
[the shared-parser issue](https://github.com/lp/mega-kb/issues/1) and
[the booking-v3 hint issue](https://github.com/lp/mega-kb/issues/2) separate. Neither is fixed
merely because the desk marked the merged ticket known.

This review uses the KB export dated 2026-10-04. The desk continues working: these are snapshot
counts, not a claim about today's live queue. Refresh the export and recalculate before using
these counts for a later handoff. A new report with an unknown tenant must remain unplaced until
the directory supplies enough evidence; a subject match is not permission to merge it.

## What the running KB shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This unchanged exported table describes the desk's original filing: “no ticket of its own”
means no separate ticket in that export. The new GitHub issues linked above and below provide
the separate tracking that was missing.

The shared parser always reads month first. Day-first tenant dates entering that path can
reverse month and day. In contrast, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, and its tenant-order form can
read a date entered by that hint in the opposite order. Changing only the shared parser does
not repair the hint/form contract.

For example, `R-41205` is from morrow.tax (`fr-FR`, booking-v2, app) despite an `en-US` browser and
an intake guess of hint. `R-41230` is from copper.studio (`es-ES`, booking-v3, form) despite an
intake guess of parser. Read the tenant directory and the entry channel, not the intake guess.
`causes.ts` asks `Intl.DateTimeFormat` for date order instead of maintaining a country list.

<ReviewEvidence />

## The queue did not begin when the desk merged it

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The two series measure different events. Received dates describe incoming reports; merge dates
describe filing. A large merge batch cannot date the beginning of the incident. Continued reports
also make “known” a workflow label, not evidence of resolution.

## Why the old dedupe rule concealed the hint defect

The [triage prose](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) say to merge by normalized subject.
Their old review date triggers the layout's stale warning. More importantly, the live component
below computes cause equality, not subject equality. The written rule and running behavior disagree.

<DedupeExplorer />

The examples show both failure directions: the same subject can describe different causes,
and different subjects can describe the same cause. Alder Physio's form and app reports share
a subject but enter different defect paths. App versus email at Kettle Books uses different
subjects but the same parser cause. With no tenant on file, the component declines to establish
a common cause.

[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) replaces the subject-only rule for this
reassessment. Decision 0001 remains intact as an accepted historical record. Use subject matches
only to find candidates, then check tenant locale, booking form, channel and symptom evidence.
Merge only reports with an established common cause; keep unresolved evidence visible.

## Handoff and limits

The linked issues each carry their report count, scope, examples and independent fix boundary.
Their reproductions are derived from the export's implementation and reports; production parser
and form code were not supplied, so no production reproduction or deployed fix is claimed.

The counts and membership above are computed by components from deduplicated data. The page source,
components, exported inputs and rendered HTML are kept together so another reader can reproduce
the evidence instead of trusting a manually copied total. The desk reference below is derived
from this page's frontmatter signature by the supplied `RenderStamp`, not typed into the prose.

